<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maranao people</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Maranaos</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Maranao are a Muslim people of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantustan">homeland</a> is the region around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-lanao">Lake Lanao</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindanao">Mindanao</a>; their name means 'people of the lake'. They accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> from the 16th century and organised themselves into many small sultanates that long resisted Spanish and American rule. The Maranao are known for brass work, weaving, the okir style of decorative carving and the epic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darangen">Darangen</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maranao-people.jpg" alt="Maranao people" width="320" height="456" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: วัศย์รุจ ปริญญาวุฒิชัย · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maranao_performing_Singkil.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
